    modelEssay: "The chart compares worldwide cinema box office revenues in billions of dollars with active subscription video-on-demand memberships (scaled in tens of millions) between 2014 and 2024.\n\nOverall, theatrical box office receipts were characterized by moderate initial gains followed by a catastrophic collapse and partial recovery around 2020. Conversely, digital streaming subscriptions experienced exponential, uninterrupted growth.\n\nBetween 2014 and 2018, theatrical exhibition thrived, with global box office climbing from $36 billion to a high of $41 billion. However, widespread theater closures in 2020 caused revenues to plummet by more than two-thirds to $12 billion. Although cinema attendance subsequently rebounded to $26 billion in 2022 and $33 billion in 2024, revenues remained below their pre-crisis peak.\n\nIn stark contrast, video streaming expanded uninterruptedly. From 120 million accounts in 2014, subscriber numbers doubled to 240 million in 2016 and reached 450 million in 2018. During the 2020 lockdowns, subscriptions surged dramatically to 950 million, continuing upward to reach 1.45 billion active memberships by 2024.",
