    suggestedTimeMinutes: 20,
    minWordCount: 150,
    modelEssay: "The climate chart illustrates monthly mean temperatures in degrees Celsius and total precipitation in millimetres for Sydney, Australia, sampled every two months across a calendar year.\n\nOverall, Sydney experiences warm summers and mild winters, with rainfall distributed throughout the year but peaking noticeably during the late summer and autumn months.\n\nTemperatures follow a classic Southern Hemisphere seasonal cycle. Peak warmth occurs in January with an average high of 26\u00b0C, dipping slightly to 25\u00b0C in March. As autumn transitions into winter, temperatures decline to 20\u00b0C in May, bottoming out in July at 16\u00b0C. Thereafter, spring brings a steady warming trend to 20\u00b0C in September and 24\u00b0C in November.\n\nPrecipitation patterns show substantial seasonal variance. Rainfall is heaviest during late summer and autumn, peaking at 130 mm in March after recording 105 mm in January and 100 mm in May. The mid-year months are markedly drier, with July logging the lowest rainfall at 60 mm, before precipitation recovers to 70 mm in September and 85 mm in November.",
