    imageUrl: "/task1/task1_057.svg",
    suggestedTimeMinutes: 20,
    minWordCount: 150,
    modelEssay: "The schematic diagram depicts the four operational stages employed in a seawater reverse osmosis desalination facility to generate potable freshwater from the ocean.\n\nOverall, the facility relies on physical intake screening, chemical and particulate pre-treatment, high-pressure membrane separation, and post-treatment remineralization, alongside the safe disposal of concentrated brine.\n\nThe operation commences at the ocean seabed, where large submerged pumps extract raw seawater through coarse protective grates that prevent marine life ingestion. The intake water is conveyed into pre-treatment filtration units, where dual-media sand filters and fine micro-cartridges strip out suspended silt, organic debris, and microscopic algae that could foul sensitive equipment.\n\nIn the core desalination stage, high-pressure industrial pumps elevate water pressure to roughly 70 bar, forcing it against spiral-wound semi-permeable membranes. These membranes allow water molecules to permeate while blocking salt ions and minerals. The resulting purified permeate is balanced with calcium and magnesium for human consumption and piped into the city network, while the rejected hypersaline brine is pumped back into deep offshore currents.",
