    modelEssay: "The two maps compare the structural arrangement of Grange Public Park upon its initial public opening in 1930 with its current modern configuration.\n\nOverall, the park evolved from a quiet, ornamental Victorian recreation ground into an active, multi-purpose family and sports community venue, incorporating dining and children's play areas.\n\nIn 1930, the northwest corner accommodated a formal fountain basin, while a rose garden was located in the northeast. At the center of the park stood a circular musical bandstand. In the southern half, visitors utilized a glass greenhouse in the southwest, an ornamental pond with seating benches in the southeast, and a southern gravel perimeter walkway.\n\nToday, the central bandstand has been reconstructed into an open-air amphitheater for musical concerts. In the north, the fountain basin was converted into an interactive children's splash park, while an outdoor cafe was integrated into the rose garden. The former glass greenhouse was removed to make way for an equipped playground, the pond expanded into a commercial boating lake, and the gravel path was paved into a dual-lane pedestrian and cycle path.",
