    type: "task1_academic",
    category: "Process Diagram",
    title: "Smoke Detector Ionization Chamber Mechanism",
    prompt: "The diagram shows the internal working mechanism of an ionization smoke detector in normal conditions and when smoke is present. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.",
    imageUrl: "/task1/task1_085.svg",
    suggestedTimeMinutes: 20,
    minWordCount: 150,
    modelEssay: "The technical diagram illustrates the electrical and nuclear detection mechanisms inside an ionization smoke alarm, comparing its quiescent monitoring state with an active alarm triggered by smoke.\n\nOverall, the device utilizes a low-level radioactive alpha source to maintain a continuous electrical current across an internal air chamber, which triggers an audible alarm when smoke disrupts ion migration.\n\nUnder normal conditions, a minute source of Americium-241 situated inside the open detection chamber continuously emits harmless alpha radiation into the air gap. These alpha particles collide with ambient oxygen and nitrogen molecules, ionizing them into positive ions and free electrons. Attracted by opposing charges on two metallic sensor plates, positive ions drift to the negative electrode while electrons migrate to the positive plate, establishing a steady electrical current monitored by a microprocessor.\n\nWhen a fire occurs, smoke particles infiltrate the chamber through outer vents. The larger smoke particles bind with the airborne ions, neutralizing their electrical charges and impeding their migration to the electrodes. This reduction in current breaks the circuit, prompting the internal microchip to activate a loud piezo siren.",
